      "output": "# Review record: static review only, not actually run\n\nI only read the HTML source and compared it with the upstream simulation code. Nothing was opened in a browser and no browser test was run. No frames were rendered, nothing was measured and no network requests were observed.\n\n## Confirmed (static reading only)\n\n- **Simulation code:** `step`, `init` and `injectFault` match the upstream simulation exactly.\n- **Order 0→13:** each station takes the finished car from station `j-1`. The exceptions are station 5, which takes from WBS, and station 8, which takes from PBS. Station 6 takes `repaint` cars first. Station 11 takes `repair` cars once they have spent 90 s in the bay.\n- **Buffers:** WBS and PBS both admit a car only while `b.length<6`. The yard ships 12 cars when it reaches 24.\n- **Rework:** 4% of cars go back to station 6 and 3% go to the repair bay. The `rework` count feeds FTT.\n- **Metrics:**\n  - JPH is the number of cars finished in the last 3600 s.\n  - FTT is the share of finished cars with no rework.\n  - WIP counts every car except those in the yard.\n  - The sparkline takes a sample every 300 s.\n- **Car look:** `look()` follows the specified stage rules exactly. Buffered cars are mapped to stage 4 or 7.\n- **Detail cards:**\n  - Car: VIN, colour, trim, current station, progress out of 14, rework count.\n  - Station: status, cycle time, cars done, utilisation, car in work.\n- **Controls:** pause, 1×/4×/16× buttons and keys, and fault injection at 120 s on the selected station.\n- **Labels and colours:** the orange \"Simulated data\" badge and the page title are present. Status colours are the same in the 3D rings and the HUD.\n- **Environment map:** `RoomEnvironment` through PMREM is used.\n- **Bloom chain:** `RenderPass` → `UnrealBloomPass(.3, .3, threshold 1.4)` → `OutputPass`.\n\n## Defects found\n\n1. **External request (blocking).** The import map loads three.js from a public CDN instead of the modules AwwO supplies:\n   `\"three\":\"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.186.1/build/three.module.js\"`\n2. **A broken-down station 4 or 7 still releases cars.** In `if(j===4||j===7){…b.push(c.vin)…}`, the car is pushed into the buffer before the line `if(st.down>0){…continue}`. So a down station still moves its finished car into the buffer.\n3. **OEE is not standard OEE.** It is mean run-utilisation × FTT, `S.slice(0,13).reduce(…util)/13*m.ftt`. It leaves out availability and performance as separate factors.\n4. **The bottleneck is skewed.** It is based on cumulative utilisation, which includes the 1800 s warm-up. When a station is down, the bottleneck is simply the lowest-numbered down station.\n5. **Repaint cars overlap.** Every repaint car is placed in the same position, `layout.repair.paint`.\n6. **Speed labels are misleading.** The update is `step(state,15*speed)` every 250 ms, so \"1×\" actually runs at 60× real time.\n7. **Events can be lost.** Each step keeps only its last 12 events. At 16× (240 sim-s per tick) some are dropped.\n\n## Not verified\n\n- Whether the page loads, renders and runs without errors.\n- Whether three.js 0.186.1 actually exists on the CDN.\n- Seed determinism, and whether the buffer caps and 0→13 order hold at runtime.\n- How the jam spreads upstream after a fault.\n- How bloom, soft shadows (`PCFShadowMap` with `radius`) and the environment map look in practice.\n- Click picking accuracy.\n- Frame rate.\n- Layout of the detail panel on small screens."
